module gunbc.auth.approval_broker_marker

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, List }
import std.markup { MarkupComment, serialize_fragment_with }
import extdeps.languages.html { html_markup_medium }
import std.markup { Serialized, Rejected }
import extdeps.http.server { MediaType, ServeResponseHeader, ServeResponseHeaderField, HttpExtensionFieldNameAdmitted, HttpExtensionFieldNameRefused, ServeWireHeader, http_extension_field_name }
import gunbc.auth.approval_writer_authority { ApprovalServingProcess, RoadmapProcess, BrokerProcess }

// ── WHICH FOLD ANSWERED, WRITTEN INTO THE ONLY CHANNEL THE PROBE CAN READ ────────────────────
// THE CUTOVER'S STAGE-5 PROBE COULD NOT TELL A BROKER 404 FROM A PREFIX-STRIPPED MISS, and that is
// the one fact the whole staged transaction turns on (cutover HOLD item 4). RouteClassAnswered
// carried a bare status. A 404 through the front door has at least three causes that a status
// cannot separate: the front door never routed the path to the broker at all; the front door routed
// it but presented `/<capability>` with the mount prefix stripped, so the broker's table saw a path
// it has no route for; or the broker genuinely lacks the route. The first says the repoint did not
// take, the second says it took and the path contract broke, the third says the extraction is
// incomplete -- three different repairs, and the operator was being asked to guess which from one
// integer. That is DESIGN's not-met versus cannot-tell collapsed into a number.
//
// THE MARKER NAMES THE PROCESS AS WELL AS THE FOLD, AND THE FIRST CUT NAMED ONLY THE FOLD -- WHICH
// DID NOT DISCRIMINATE WHAT IT CLAIMED TO (review 68539). Until stage 6 the roadmap MOUNTS THESE
// SAME FOUR FOLDS: gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_serve projects its approval rows from
// approval_broker_route_specs and invokes approval_broker_invoke with process: RoadmapProcess, so a
// handler-only token is emitted verbatim by the process the probe exists to rule out. A front-door
// probe answered by the roadmap carried approval-broker/confirm-page, agreed with the expected
// marker, and was ADMITTED -- the precise dangerous reading the refusal prose says is refused, and
// the stage's whole load-bearing inference. The handler arm is a fact about which ROUTE matched; it
// was never a fact about WHICH PROCESS ANSWERED.
//
// THE PROCESS IS AVAILABLE AND WAS SIMPLY NOT CARRIED. Both serving processes already thread an
// ApprovalServingProcess into approval_broker_invoke, bound by the serve ENTRY -- one entry per
// process for its life -- so it is the one value in the request path that a response cannot lie
// about by matching a route. Marking on the pair (process, fold) is what makes the token a
// statement about the answering process.
//
// SO EVERY RESPONSE NAMES THE PROCESS AND THE FOLD THAT PRODUCED IT. A 404 marked
// broker/no-route-matched says the request REACHED THE BROKER and its table refused it -- which, on
// a path the broker demonstrably routes, is the prefix-strip reading and nothing else. A 404 marked
// roadmap/anything says the front door is still pointing at the old process. A body with no marker
// at all says neither served it. The discriminator is the PROCESS SEGMENT plus the presence of a
// marker, never the status.
//
// THE CHANNEL IS A FACT ABOUT THE MEDIA TYPE (see approval_broker_marked_body): a format with comment
// syntax carries the marker in its body, every other format carries it in the X-Served-By response
// header (admitted by extdeps.http.server http_extension_field_name; the name and token are this
// module's, X-Served-By being a gunbc convention and not an IANA-registered field). A first cut put it in the body of EVERY
// response, which made application/json bodies unparseable to the phone app.

// THE LAST THREE ARMS ARE NOT HANDLERS, and they are here because the invariant is about the
// PROCESS and not about the four routes. BrokerAdapterRefused covers what the adapter answers
// before any route is selected (an unparseable method, a route table that refused its own
// literals); BrokerMethodNotAllowed and BrokerNoRouteMatched cover selection outcomes;
// BrokerStaticRouteServed covers a static row served straight from the table. Without them the
// match that must mark every response would have arms with nothing to name, and an unmarked body
// from any one of them reads to the probe as "the broker never saw this request" -- the one
// inference the marker exists to make sound, wrong in the direction that refuses a correct cutover.
type ApprovalBrokerMarker
  = SubmissionHandlerObserved
  | StatusHandlerObserved
  | StoreReceiptHandlerObserved
  | DeviceEnrolHandlerObserved
  | DeviceRedeemHandlerObserved
  | DevicePendingHandlerObserved
  | DeviceRequestHandlerObserved
  | DeviceEnrollmentReadbackHandlerObserved
  | DevicePushUpdateHandlerObserved
  | DeviceListHandlerObserved
  | DeviceRevokeHandlerObserved
  | BrokerNoRouteMatched
  | BrokerMethodNotAllowed
  | BrokerAdapterRefused
  | BrokerStaticRouteServed

// IT IS A TOKEN, NOT A LABEL, AND THE NAME IS THE FIX (review 68760). This fold was
// approval_serving_process_label -- the same name, the same signature, over the same
// ApprovalServingProcess, as gunbc.auth.approval_writer_authority's fold ONE IMPORT EDGE AWAY,
// which returns operator prose ("the roadmap serve process (gunbc-roadmap.service)"). One name,
// two materially different meanings: DESIGN section 3's meaning fork, the dual of nicknaming.
//
// THE STAKES WERE THIS MODULE'S OWN. approval_broker_served_token is the SOLE discriminator the
// stage-5 probe uses to tell the broker from the roadmap -- the hole review 68539 exists to close.
// An edit resolving the authority module's fold here would silently change the emitted token and
// reopen it, with nothing in the type system objecting because the signatures agree. Sharper than
// the review's reading: .dag names resolve corpus-wide rather than by import list, so WHICH of two
// identically-named folds wins is not settled by the braces at the top of this file.
//
// NEITHER TOKEN IS A PREFIX OF THE OTHER, which is what keeps the substring reader below from
// answering `broker` on a roadmap-served body. That is a property of these two spellings and not
// of the scheme, so it is stated where a third process would be added.
fn approval_serving_process_token(p: ApprovalServingProcess) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match p {
    RoadmapProcess => "roadmap" as NonEmptyStr
    BrokerProcess => "broker" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// THESE ARE BARE FOLD NAMES AND ARE NEVER MATCHED ON DIRECTLY. `status` or `decision` alone would
// match prose inside a refusal reason and read as a marker; the `approval-broker/<process>/` prefix
// that approval_broker_served_token puts in front is what makes the observation about this corpus
// AND about one process. A reader matching these strings would reproduce review 68539's defect.
//
// THESE TWO ANNOTATIONS WERE ATTACHED TO EACH OTHER'S FOLDS until review 68760 sent me back here:
// the process-token fold was inserted above this one during the process-segment repair, and each
// comment stayed where it was, so each described its neighbour. Recorded rather than silently
// swapped because it is the failure mode DESIGN section 4c exists to bound -- an annotation is
// unreadable by any Accepted program, so nothing but a reader can notice it has drifted onto the
// wrong declaration.
fn approval_broker_marker_token(m: ApprovalBrokerMarker) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match m {
    SubmissionHandlerObserved => "submission" as NonEmptyStr
    StatusHandlerObserved => "status" as NonEmptyStr
    StoreReceiptHandlerObserved => "store-receipt" as NonEmptyStr
    DeviceEnrolHandlerObserved => "device-enrol" as NonEmptyStr
    DeviceRedeemHandlerObserved => "device-redeem" as NonEmptyStr
    DevicePendingHandlerObserved => "device-pending" as NonEmptyStr
    DeviceRequestHandlerObserved => "device-request" as NonEmptyStr
    DeviceEnrollmentReadbackHandlerObserved => "device-enrollment-readback" as NonEmptyStr
    DevicePushUpdateHandlerObserved => "device-push-update" as NonEmptyStr
    DeviceListHandlerObserved => "device-list" as NonEmptyStr
    DeviceRevokeHandlerObserved => "device-revoke" as NonEmptyStr
    BrokerNoRouteMatched => "no-route-matched" as NonEmptyStr
    BrokerMethodNotAllowed => "method-not-allowed" as NonEmptyStr
    BrokerAdapterRefused => "adapter-refused" as NonEmptyStr
    BrokerStaticRouteServed => "static-route" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data approval_broker_markers: List<ApprovalBrokerMarker> = [
  SubmissionHandlerObserved,
  StatusHandlerObserved, StoreReceiptHandlerObserved,
  DeviceEnrolHandlerObserved, DeviceRedeemHandlerObserved, DevicePendingHandlerObserved,
  DeviceRequestHandlerObserved, DeviceEnrollmentReadbackHandlerObserved, DevicePushUpdateHandlerObserved,
  DeviceListHandlerObserved, DeviceRevokeHandlerObserved,
  BrokerNoRouteMatched, BrokerMethodNotAllowed, BrokerAdapterRefused,
  BrokerStaticRouteServed,
]

// THE TOKEN THE WIRE CARRIES IS THE PAIR. approval_broker_marker_token alone remains the fold's own
// name and is NOT what a probe matches on: a reader that matched it would accept either process,
// which is exactly the defect review 68539 found.
fn approval_broker_served_token(p: ApprovalServingProcess, m: ApprovalBrokerMarker) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([
    "approval-broker/",
    approval_serving_process_token(p: p) as String,
    "/",
    approval_broker_marker_token(m: m) as String,
  ], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

// The HTML marker, built as the markup it is rather than as the characters around it. Spelling
// "<!-- " + token + " -->" here put a language's comment delimiters in an auth module (a second copy
// beside extdeps.languages.html's rows, §3) and interpolated an unvalidated value into the middle of
// them: a token containing "-->" would close the comment early and the rest would be parsed as markup
// in a confirmation page. Building a MarkupComment and asking the serializer for the characters takes
// BOTH the delimiters and that check out of this module: serialize_fragment_with consults the medium's
// CommentGrammar on the arm that writes a comment, so a fragment route is checked as surely as a
// document route -- review 82279 on #14351 removed the walk-then-serialize pairing this function used
// to do by hand. The refusal is therefore not the only thing standing between a bad token and a served
// page: approval_broker_served_token is a join of three closed token sets (lowercase, digits, "/", "-")
// so the check cannot fire on it today, and the claim witness_marker_token_never_closes_its_comment
// establishes that as a checked fact over every inhabitant of both closed sets. If a future marker name ever contained the terminator, this
// returns the diagnostic instead of shipping a page whose marker is markup.
// A CLOSED RESULT, not a string that means either thing. Returning the diagnostic as the comment text
// was a fabricated output (review 82142 on #14351): the caller joined whatever came back into the
// served body, so a firing hazard would have shipped a page whose marker line was an error message and
// whose marker was simply absent — an unmarked page that the READING side then calls "the request never
// reached the broker", which is the one question this marker exists to settle. The refusal has to be
// un-forgeable at the type, so the caller cannot join it into a body by accident.
type BrokerMarkerComment
  = MarkerCommentRendered { text: String }
  | MarkerCommentRefused { reason: String }

// ONE CALL, NOT A PAIR. This used to run the walk and then the serializer by hand, which is the shape
// review 82279 on #14351 pointed at: two calls that a caller must remember to make together, and a
// serializer that would happily write the comment if called alone. The serializer is now the check, so
// there is nothing to pair and no way to get the bytes without passing it.
fn approval_broker_marker_comment(body: String) -> BrokerMarkerComment {
  match serialize_fragment_with(frag: MarkupComment { body: body }, medium: html_markup_medium()) {
    Serialized { html: text } => MarkerCommentRendered { text: text }
    Rejected { reason: why } => MarkerCommentRefused { reason: why }
  }
}

// ── HOW IT IS CARRIED, WHICH IS A FACT ABOUT THE MEDIA TYPE AND NOT ABOUT THE HANDLER ───────
// A MARKER MAY NOT CORRUPT THE FORMAT IT MARKS. HTML has a comment (invisible on a phone, still a
// substring of the bytes a probe fetches) and JavaScript has a `//` line, so those two carry the
// marker in the body. EVERY OTHER SUBTYPE -- application/json first, but also text/plain, whose
// consumers parse the whole body (a receipt, a status line) -- has no comment syntax, so appending
// to its body changes the value the client reads. Those carry it in the X-Served-By response header
// instead: the body is untouched and the marker still rides the one response. The earlier arm
// appended `x-approval-broker: <token>` to the end of the body of those formats; captured live, a
// device-enrol refusal came back as `{...}x-approval-broker: ...`, which no strict JSON decoder reads.
//
// IT DISPATCHES ON THE SUBTYPE AND NOT ON THE MediaType VALUE, because MediaType is a RECORD --
// a top level, a subtype and parameters -- and not a closed variant. The subtype decides whether
// comment syntax exists, which is the actual question.
//
// A media type with no comment syntax is still MARKED, never left bare: an unmarked response is what
// the reading side calls "the request never reached the broker".
// The CLOSED RESULT of marking. BrokerBodyMarked carries the body to serve and the headers that carry
// the marker (empty when the body does). BrokerMarkerRefused is a construction failure that the serve
// fold turns into a typed 500 through the route a page-render refusal already takes
// (broker_text_response). A caller cannot join a BrokerMarkerRefused into a body: there is no String.
type BrokerMarkedBody
  = BrokerBodyMarked { body: String, headers: List<ServeResponseHeader> }
  | BrokerMarkerRefused { reason: String }

// The response header a non-comment-capable format carries the served-by token in, and the one name
// the reader looks for.
data approval_broker_marker_header_name: String = "X-Served-By"

fn approval_broker_marked_body(content_type: MediaType, body: String, m: ApprovalBrokerMarker, process: ApprovalServingProcess) -> BrokerMarkedBody {
  let token = approval_broker_served_token(p: process, m: m)
  if content_type.subtype == "html" {
    match approval_broker_marker_comment(body: token as String) {
      MarkerCommentRendered { text: c } => BrokerBodyMarked { body: join([body, "\n", c, "\n"], ""), headers: [] }
      MarkerCommentRefused { reason: r } => BrokerMarkerRefused { reason: r }
    }
  } else if content_type.subtype == "javascript" {
    BrokerBodyMarked { body: join([body, "\n// x-approval-broker: ", token as String, "\n"], ""), headers: [] }
  } else {
    match http_extension_field_name(label: approval_broker_marker_header_name) {
      HttpExtensionFieldNameAdmitted { name: n } => BrokerBodyMarked { body: body, headers: [ServeResponseHeaderField { name: n, value: token as String }] }
      HttpExtensionFieldNameRefused { cause: r } => BrokerMarkerRefused { reason: r }
    }
  }
}

// ── THE READING SIDE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE PROBE SUPPLIES WHAT IT SAW AND THIS FOLD SAYS WHAT IT MEANS, so no caller re-implements the
// substring test and no two callers disagree about it. It takes the body a fetch returned; it does
// not fetch, because a fold that fetched would be observing and adjudicating in one place and the
// cutover's whole shape is that observations are supplied to admission (DESIGN section 3d:
// selection precedes convergence).
// IT CARRIES THE PROCESS, and that field is the repair for review 68539 rather than extra detail.
// An ObservedMarker holding only the fold cannot answer the question the stage asks -- which process
// served this -- so every consumer would have had to guess, and the one that mattered guessed the
// answer it wanted.
type ObservedMarker
  = NoMarkerInBody
  | MarkerObserved { process: ApprovalServingProcess, marker: ApprovalBrokerMarker }

data approval_serving_processes: List<ApprovalServingProcess> = [RoadmapProcess, BrokerProcess]

// THE SCAN IS OVER THE PAIR, so a roadmap-served body can only ever produce a MarkerObserved whose
// process is RoadmapProcess. Scanning the fold names alone is what admitted the roadmap's answer.
fn approval_broker_marker_in_text(body: String) -> ObservedMarker {
  fold(approval_serving_processes, init: NoMarkerInBody, f: (outer, p) =>
    match outer {
      MarkerObserved { process: _, marker: _ } => outer
      NoMarkerInBody =>
        fold(approval_broker_markers, init: NoMarkerInBody, f: (acc, m) =>
          match acc {
            MarkerObserved { process: _, marker: _ } => acc
            NoMarkerInBody =>
              if string_contains(s: body, pattern: approval_broker_served_token(p: p, m: m) as String) {
                MarkerObserved { process: p, marker: m }
              } else {
                NoMarkerInBody
              }
          }
        )
    }
  )
}

// THE READER LOOKS WHERE THE PRODUCER PUT IT: the X-Served-By header values first, then the body.
// Only a header NAMED X-Served-By counts, so a body or another header that happens to quote a token
// cannot stand for the channel, and a header-carried marker is found on a JSON route whose body is
// untouched. A response with neither is NoMarkerInBody.
fn approval_broker_marker_in_response(headers: List<ServeWireHeader>, body: String) -> ObservedMarker {
  let served_by = headers |> flat_map(h => if h.name == approval_broker_marker_header_name { [h.value] } else { [] })
  match approval_broker_marker_in_text(body: join(served_by, "\n")) {
    MarkerObserved { process: p, marker: m } => MarkerObserved { process: p, marker: m }
    NoMarkerInBody => approval_broker_marker_in_text(body: body)
  }
}

// ── AGREEMENT AS A VALUE, BECAUSE A BARE `if` CANNOT PRODUCE AN Optional HERE ────────────────
// The comparison used to sit inline in gunbc.auth.approval_broker_cutover route_class_admission,
// with `Absent` in one branch and a `Present` in the other, and it did not typecheck: the branches
// resolved to Optional and CutoverRefusal rather than to one type. That is the same shape
// ReleaseCandidateMatch already fixed in that module, so it gets the same repair rather than a
// second idiom -- the agreement is decided here, as a value, and the caller's `match` is what
// produces the Optional.
//
// IT COMPARES SERVED TOKENS AND NOT ARMS because the served token IS the identity that crosses the
// wire, and it is the PAIR: comparing fold arms alone is the defect review 68539 found, where the
// roadmap's own answer agreed with the expected marker because only the route had to match.
// Comparing what the probe actually reads keeps the claim about the observable.
type MarkerAgreement
  = MarkerAgrees
  | MarkerDiffers

fn approval_broker_marker_agreement(
  observed_process: ApprovalServingProcess,
  observed: ApprovalBrokerMarker,
  expected_process: ApprovalServingProcess,
  expected: ApprovalBrokerMarker,
) -> MarkerAgreement {
  if approval_broker_served_token(p: observed_process, m: observed)
    == approval_broker_served_token(p: expected_process, m: expected) {
    MarkerAgrees
  } else {
    MarkerDiffers
  }
}
